{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1461 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "3d2c7cd2777b8123c1cd5f938e7567229d2e131ddeaca107f5449f04b77deadf" :edb/db-before-t 1460 :edb/db-after-t 1461 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534773 50 #inst "2026-10-08T06:29:52.325Z" 13194139534773 true] [17592186128801 1100 "Consider the collection of point masses $m_1=1\\,\\textrm{kg}$, $m_2=2\\,\\textrm{kg}$, $m_3=3\\,\\textrm{kg}$, and $m_4=3\\,\\textrm{kg}$ located at $x_1=-1\\,\\textrm{m}$, $x_2 = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$, $x_3 = 3\\,\\textrm{m}$, and $x_4 = 5 \\, \\textrm{m}$, respectively, along the $x$-axis. Find the total moment of this system about the origin." 13194139534773 false] [17592186128801 1100 "Consider the collection of point masses $m_1=1\\,\\textrm{kg},$ $m_2=2\\,\\textrm{kg},$ $m_3=3\\,\\textrm{kg},$ and $m_4=3\\,\\textrm{kg}$ located at $x_1=-1\\,\\textrm{m},$ $x_2 = 1\\,\\textrm{m},$ $x_3 = 3\\,\\textrm{m},$ and $x_4 = 5 \\, \\textrm{m},$ respectively, along the $x$-axis. Find the total moment of this system about the origin." 13194139534773 true] [17592186128801 1145 "The moment about the origin $O$ of a collection of masses $m_1,m_2,\\ldots,m_n$ located at $x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n$ along the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nM_O = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i x_i\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}m_{1} &= 1, & m_{2}|= 2, & m_{3}|= 3, & m_{4}|= 3, \\\\ x_{1}|=-1, & x_{2}|= 1, & x_{3}|= 3, & x_{4}|= 5\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the total moment of this system about the origin as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{O} &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)m_{i}x_{i} \\\\\n&= m_{1}x_{1} + m_{2}x_{2} + m_{3}x_{3} + m_{4}x_{4} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot (-1) + 2 \\cdot 1 + 3 \\cdot 3 + 3 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&=-1 + 2 + 9 + 15 \\\\\n&= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total moment of the system is $25\\,\\textrm{kg} \\cdot \\textrm{m}$." 13194139534773 false] [17592186128801 1145 "The moment about the origin $O$ of a collection of masses $m_1,m_2,\\ldots,m_n$ located at $x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n$ along the $x$-axis is given by \n\n$$\nM_O = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i x_i.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =1, & \\,{m}_{2} & =2, & \\,{m}_{3} & =3, & \\,{m}_{4} & =3, \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-1, & \\,{x}_{2} & =1, & \\,{x}_{3} & =3, & \\,{x}_{4} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the total moment of this system about the origin as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3}+{m}_{4}{x}_{4} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-1+2+9+15 \\\\  & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the total moment of the system is $25\\,\\textrm{kg} \\cdot \\textrm{m}.$" 13194139534773 true] [17592186128802 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033857/assets/cea41dc5efa961e4d0e3ccf42db21113d3b7d1d2240f3edbcc4c1f0cab61ead1.png)\n\nConsider the collection of point masses above, where the $x$-coordinates are measured in meters. What is the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass for this system?" 13194139534773 true] [17592186128802 1100 "Consider the collection of point masses above, where the $x$-coordinates are measured in meters. What is the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass for this system?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2026/Source/Images/7d1080a4e47e1448ba5bd6fef71f207e.png)" 13194139534773 false] [17592186128802 1145 "The center of mass, denoted $\\overline{x}$, of a collection of point masses $m_1,m_2,\\ldots,m_n$ located at $x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n$ along the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{M_O}{m}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\displaystyle m = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n m_i$ is the total mass of the system,\n- $\\displaystyle M_O = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i x_i$ is the total moment of the system about the origin $O$.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}m_{1} &= 2, & m_{2}|= 4, & m_{3}|= 3, \\\\ x_{1}|=-1, & x_{2}|= 2, & x_{3}|= 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)m_{i} \\\\\n&= m_{1} + m_{2} + m_{3} \\\\\n&= 2 + 4 + 3 \\\\\n&= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the total moment of the system about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{O} &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)m_{i}x_{i} \\\\\n&= m_{1}x_{1} + m_{2}x_{2} + m_{3}x_{3} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot (-1) + 4 \\cdot 2 + 3 \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&=-2 + 8 + 12 \\\\\n&= 18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{M_O}{m} = \\dfrac{18}{9}=2\n$$" 13194139534773 false] [17592186128802 1145 "The center of mass, denoted $\\overline{x},$ of a collection of point masses $m_1,m_2,\\ldots,m_n$ located at $x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n$ along the $x$-axis is given by \n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{M_O}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $\\displaystyle m = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n m_i$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_O = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i x_i$ is the total moment of the system about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =2, & \\,{m}_{2} & =4, & \\,{m}_{3} & =3, \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-1, & \\,{x}_{2} & =2, & \\,{x}_{3} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =2+4+3 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the total moment of the system about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-2+8+12 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{M_O}{m} = \\dfrac{18}{9}=2.\n$$" 13194139534773 true] [17592186128803 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033857/assets/0e4802c6642fc0950358235f30fb8c994d26b912e557122af08b99277bb6192a.png)\n\nConsider the collection of point masses shown above. Given that the center of mass for the collection is located at $(\\overline{x}, \\overline{y}),$ what is the value of $\\overline{y}?$" 13194139534773 true] [17592186128803 1100 "Consider the collection of point masses shown above. Given that the center of mass for the collection is located at $(\\overline{x}, \\overline{y})$, what is the value of $\\overline{y}$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2026/Source/Images/a98849cc62e6b254955495d1c98f4f85.png)" 13194139534773 false] [17592186128803 1145 "The center of mass of a collection of point masses $m_1,m_2,\\ldots,m_n$ located at $(x_1y_1),(x_2,y_2),\\ldots,(x_n,y_n)$ on the plane is given by\n\n$$\n(\\overline{x},\\overline{y}) = \\left(\\dfrac{M_y}{m},\\dfrac{M_x}{m}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\displaystyle m = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n m_i$ is the total mass of the system,\n- $\\displaystyle M_x = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i y_i$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n- $\\displaystyle M_y = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i x_i$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis.\n\nLet's denote the masses as\n\n$$\nm_1 = 3, \\qquad m_2 = 5, \\qquad m_3 = 1\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $y$-coordinates of the masses are\n\n$$\ny_1 = 3, \\qquad y_2 = 0, \\qquad y_3 = 3\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)m_{i} \\\\\n&= m_{1} + m_{2} + m_{3} \\\\\n&= 3 + 5 + 1 \\\\\n&= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM_{x} &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)m_{i}y_{i} \\\\\n&= m_{1}y_{1} + m_{2}y_{2} + m_{3}y_{3} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 3 + 5 \\cdot 0 + 1 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= 9 + 0 + 3 \\\\\n&= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\overline{y} = \\dfrac{M_x}{m} = \\dfrac{12}{9} = \\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$" 13194139534773 false] [17592186128803 1145 "The center of mass of a collection of point masses $m_1,m_2,\\ldots,m_n$ located at $(x_1y_1),(x_2,y_2),\\ldots,(x_n,y_n)$ on the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overline{x},\\overline{y}) = \\left(\\dfrac{M_y}{m},\\dfrac{M_x}{m}\\right),\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $\\displaystyle m = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n m_i$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_x = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i y_i$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_y = \\sum_{i=1}^n m_i x_i$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis.\n\nLet's denote the masses as\n\n$$\nm_1 = 3, \\qquad m_2 = 5, \\qquad m_3 = 1.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $y$-coordinates of the masses are\n\n$$\ny_1 = 3, \\qquad y_2 = 0, \\qquad y_3 = 3.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =3+5+1 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{y}_{1}+{m}_{2}{y}_{2}+{m}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3+5\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =9+0+3 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overline{y} = \\dfrac{M_x}{m} = \\dfrac{12}{9} = \\dfrac{4}{3}.\n$$" 13194139534773 true] [17592186128804 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033857/assets/440f58e3d5e652374689083ffa672f505888259a51e1c5ef2207d94410eb8cab.png)\n\n     \n\nTwo point masses $A$ and $B$ are connected by a rigid, massless rod of length $9\\,\\rm{ft}$ that is perfectly balanced on a fulcrum, as shown above. If $B$ has mass $10\\,\\rm{lb}$ and is $4\\,\\rm{ft}$ from the fulcrum, find the mass of $A.$" 13194139534773 true] [17592186128804 1100 "Two point masses $A$ and $B$ are connected by a rigid, massless rod of length $9\\,\\rm{ft}$ that is perfectly balanced on a fulcrum, as shown above. If $B$ has mass $10\\,\\rm{lb}$ and is $4\\,\\rm{ft}$ from the fulcrum, find the mass of $A$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2026/Source/Images/ce815ce5a9571e39d171d6fccd5e66bf.png)" 13194139534773 false] [17592186128804 1145 "Let $m$ be the mass of $A$.\nThe length of the rod is $9\\,\\rm{ft}$ and the distance between the fulcrum and $B$ is $4\\,\\rm{ft}$. So, the distance between the fulcrum and $A$ is\n\n$$\n9 - 4 = 5\\,\\rm{ft}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2026/Source/Images/5fbffe1d0b5c5d2ef8b9c2618163c14b.png)\n\nLet's write down the moment of each mass about the fulcrum:\n\n- The moment of mass $A$ equals $-m \\cdot 5 = -5m\\,\\textrm{lb}\\cdot\\textrm{ft}$ (negative since the motion induced by this mass would be counterclockwise).\n- The moment of mass $B$ equals $10 \\cdot 4 = 40\\,\\textrm{lb}\\cdot\\textrm{ft}$ (positive since the motion induced by this mass would be clockwise).\n\nThe *principle of moments* tells us that for the two masses to balance perfectly, the sum of the moments must equal zero.\n\nTherefore, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n40 - 5m &= 0 \\\\\n5m &= 40 \\\\\nm &= 8lb\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534773 false] [17592186128804 1145 "Let $m$ be the mass of $A.$\nThe length of the rod is $9\\,\\rm{ft}$ and the distance between the fulcrum and $B$ is $4\\,\\rm{ft}.$ So, the distance between the fulcrum and $A$ is\n\n$$\n9 - 4 = 5\\,\\rm{ft},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033857/assets/dc2503e1ed1cd91a9f3876ec5735d5c375c36b9bcac6216659a29f5efa294ea0.png)\n\nLet's write down the moment of each mass about the fulcrum:\n\n- The moment of mass $A$ equals $-m \\cdot 5 = -5m\\,\\textrm{lb}\\cdot\\textrm{ft}$ (negative since the motion induced by this mass would be counterclockwise).\n\n- The moment of mass $B$ equals $10 \\cdot 4 = 40\\,\\textrm{lb}\\cdot\\textrm{ft}$ (positive since the motion induced by this mass would be clockwise).\n\nThe *principle of moments* tells us that for the two masses to balance perfectly, the sum of the moments must equal zero.\n\nTherefore, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40-5m & =0 \\\\ 5m & =40 \\\\ m & =8\\,lb.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534773 true] [17592186129226 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309138 13194139534773 true] [17592186129226 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309145 13194139534773 true] [17592186129226 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309152 13194139534773 true] [17592186129226 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309159 13194139534773 true] [17592186129226 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309166 13194139534773 true] [17592186129227 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309173 13194139534773 true] [17592186129227 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309180 13194139534773 true] [17592186129227 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309187 13194139534773 true] [17592186129227 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309194 13194139534773 true] [17592186129228 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309201 13194139534773 true] [17592186129228 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309208 13194139534773 true] [17592186129228 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309215 13194139534773 true] [17592186129228 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309222 13194139534773 true] [17592186129228 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309229 13194139534773 true] [17592186129229 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309236 13194139534773 true] [17592186129229 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309243 13194139534773 true] [17592186129229 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309250 13194139534773 true] [17592186129477 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534773 true] [17592186129477 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033857/assets/4561f2a4671ae67720e44d334d8bf75525fd72aeee62994cfea70c300702c5b4.png)\n\nConsider the collection of point masses above, where the $x$-coordinates are measured in meters. What is the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass for this system?" 13194139534773 true] [17592186129477 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2026/Source/Images/q-163082.png)\n\nConsider the collection of point masses above, where the $x$-coordinates are measured in meters. What is the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass for this system?" 13194139534773 false] [17592186129477 1145 "The center of mass, denoted $\\overset{―}{x},$ of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ along the $x$-axis is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- ${M}_{O}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}$ is the total moment of the system about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =2, & \\,{m}_{2} & =3, & \\,{m}_{3} & =1, & \\,{m}_{4} & =5, \\\\ {x}_{1} & =1, & \\,{x}_{2} & =3, & \\,{x}_{3} & =5, & \\,{x}_{4} & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3}+{m}_{4} \\\\  & =2+3+1+5 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the total moment of the system about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3}+{m}_{4}{x}_{4} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 5+5\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =2+9+5+30 \\\\  & =46.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{46}{11}.\n$$" 13194139534773 true] [17592186129486 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534773 true] [17592186129486 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033857/assets/16e40e931919d695fe867f128827bc5351e390bb712009bfba130d195f5f35a2.png)\n\nTwo point masses $A$ and $B$ of mass $10\\,kg$ and $8kg$, respectively, are connected by a rigid, massless rod that is perfectly balanced on a fulcrum, as shown above. If the distance between the fulcrum and $B$ is $5\\,m,$ find the distance between the fulcrum and $A.$" 13194139534773 true] [17592186129486 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2026/Source/Images/q-163796.png)\n\nTwo point masses $A$ and $B$ of mass $10kg$ and $8kg$, respectively, are connected by a rigid, massless rod that is perfectly balanced on a fulcrum, as shown above. If the distance between the fulcrum and $B$ is $5m$, find the distance between the fulcrum and $A$." 13194139534773 false] [17592186129486 1145 "Let $d$ be the distance between the fulcrum and $A,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033857/assets/6df23f8b7fd4202667daa23b1dc110bb1d546516fd37a2abd208b9c1c84b79c4.png)\n\nLet's write down the moment of each mass about the fulcrum:\n\n- The moment of mass $A$ equals $-10\\cdot d=-10d\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}$ (negative since the motion induced by this mass would be counterclockwise).\n\n- The moment of mass $B$ equals $8\\cdot 5=40\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}$ (positive since the motion induced by this mass would be clockwise).\n\nThe *principle of moments* tells us that for the two masses to perfectly balance, the sum of the moments must equal zero.\n\nTherefore, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40-10d & =0 \\\\ 10d & =40 \\\\ d & =4\\,m.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534773 true] [17592186129487 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534773 true] [17592186129487 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033857/assets/f0ca128a0755c0419d03d119501210b4be8036ccc0148f21b0e3a3665def30d5.png)\n\nTwo point masses $A$ and $B$ are connected by a rigid, massless rod of length $10\\,m$ that is perfectly balanced on a fulcrum, as shown above. If $B$ has mass $3\\,kg$ and is $4\\,m$ from the fulcrum, find the mass of $A.$" 13194139534773 true] [17592186129487 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2026/Source/Images/q-163797.png)\n\nTwo point masses $A$ and $B$ are connected by a rigid, massless rod of length $10m$ that is perfectly balanced on a fulcrum, as shown above. If $B$ has mass $3kg$ and is $4m$ from the fulcrum, find the mass of $A$." 13194139534773 false] [17592186129487 1145 "Let $m$ be the mass of $A.$\nThe length of the rod is $10\\,m$ and the distance between the fulcrum and $B$ is $4\\,m.$ So, the distance between the fulcrum and $A$ is\n\n$$\n10-4=6\\,m,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033857/assets/a68789963345d21ca403d22a5f97ee440b0c3baa36345b0161bc7f80a8e0d1e0.png)\n\nLet's write down the moment of each mass about the fulcrum:\n\n- The moment of mass $A$ equals $-m\\cdot 6=-6m\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}$ (negative since the motion induced by this mass would be counterclockwise).\n\n- The moment of mass $B$ equals $3\\cdot 4=12\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}$ (positive since the motion induced by this mass would be clockwise).\n\nThe *principle of moments* tells us that for the two masses to balance perfectly, the sum of the moments must equal zero.\n\nTherefore, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-6m & =0 \\\\ 6m & =12 \\\\ m & =2\\,kg.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534773 true] [17592186309138 1017 #edb/ref 17592186309139 13194139534773 true] [17592186309138 1089 "q-163185" 13194139534773 true] [17592186309138 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534773 true] [17592186309138 1100 "Consider the collection of point masses ${m}_{1}=3\\,\\text{kg},$ ${m}_{2}=1.5\\,\\text{kg},$ ${m}_{3}=4\\,\\text{kg},$ and ${m}_{4}=2\\,\\text{kg}$ located at ${x}_{1}=1\\,\\text{m},$ ${x}_{2}=2\\,\\text{m},$ ${x}_{3}=4\\,\\text{m},$ and ${x}_{4}=3\\,\\text{m},$ respectively, along the $x$-axis. Find the total moment of this system about the origin." 13194139534773 true] [17592186309138 1113 #uuid "c92a5eca-b72e-5e6e-95be-47a34117a2da" 13194139534773 true] [17592186309138 1145 "The moment about the origin $O$ of a collection of masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ along the $x$-axis is given by \n\n$$\n{M}_{O}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =3, & \\,{m}_{2} & =1.5, & \\,{m}_{3} & =4, & \\,{m}_{4} & =2, \\\\ {x}_{1} & =1, & \\,{x}_{2} & =2, & \\,{x}_{3} & =4, & \\,{x}_{4} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the total moment of this system about the origin as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3}+{m}_{4}{x}_{4} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1+1.5\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3+3+16+6 \\\\  & =28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the total moment of the system is $28\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.$" 13194139534773 true] [17592186309139 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309140 13194139534773 true] [17592186309139 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309141 13194139534773 true] [17592186309139 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309142 13194139534773 true] [17592186309139 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309143 13194139534773 true] [17592186309139 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309144 13194139534773 true] [17592186309139 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534773 true] [17592186309139 1041 "selection" 13194139534773 true] [17592186309139 1104 #edb/ref 17592186309140 13194139534773 true] [17592186309139 1110 #uuid "9982e3e3-1fed-5e56-8c39-fa3f5f649b6f" 13194139534773 true] [17592186309140 1034 "28\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" 13194139534773 true] [17592186309140 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534773 true] [17592186309140 1111 #uuid "4495b4ea-7349-5a19-854a-7c2d696b79e2" 13194139534773 true] [17592186309141 1034 "30\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" 13194139534773 true] [17592186309141 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534773 true] [17592186309141 1111 #uuid "48894fe0-a31d-51bc-960a-85a36cabf623" 13194139534773 true] [17592186309142 1034 "25\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}" 13194139534773 true] [17592186309142 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534773 true] [17592186309142 1111 #uuid "422bdef6-b0d3-5a64-ac28-8b6ad2036061" 13194